4. The vessel started discharging the cargo on 8-3-2003 and completed the discharge on 17-3-2003. There was a joint inspection by the surveyor of the agent of the consignee, agent of the ship and Customs Preventive Officer, Chittagong, on 8-3-2003 and the report reveals that 201.699 MT of the CDSO at bond temperature were short landed for which the plaintiff incurred loss of US $ dollar 141,676.37 equivalent to Bangladeshi Taka 82,29,980.90. The defendants admitting the shortage issued P&amp;I Club guarantee through James Finlay, Chittagong, in favour of the plaintiffs through securing an amount of US $ dollar 33,869 for anticipated custom penalty and for US $ dollar 1,12,897 for consignee&#39;s cargo claim at a total of US $ dollar 1,46,766 equivalent to Bangladeshi Taka 85,25,636 at the rate of, Bangladesh Taka 58.09 per one dollar. Subsequently the agent of the vessel Pacific Ocean Ltd demanded their claim against their principle to be secured by furnishing unconditional bank guarantee from the James Finlay as the vessel is going to be scrapped in Bangladesh.&nbsp; 

 8. It has been asserted in the written statement, after denying all the material allegations brought in the plaint, that the oil tanker &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; carried the imported CDSO of the plaintiffs from the load Port Sanlorenzo to the Chittagong port. The vessel arrived at Chittagong Port on 8-3-2003, with the cargo against bills of leading for an estimated quantity of 24,000 MT. After the arrival of the vessel and before discharge the same, a joint ullage survey was carried out on board the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; as a regular action and the surveyor found the cargo at a quantum of 24,873 MT of crude degummed Soyabean Oil on board the vessel and accordingly issued ullage Survey report on 8-3-2003. The said ullage survey was conducted in presence of and attended by the surveyor on behalf of the consignee and the carrier. Thereafter, the discharge was commenced and completed on 17-3-2003, when the vessel tanks were jointly inspected and checked by the Chief Officer of the vessel and surveyor of the cargo owner and found that the tanks of the vessel is empty and dry and accordingly a dry tank certificate dated 17-3-2003 was issued. There was no short landing of 201.699 MT of the cargo rather an excess quantity of 0.873 MT of the CDSO were discharged from the vessel. The plaintiff since most illegally threatened the vessel to be detained and arrested for some alleged short landing of cargo, the owners of the vessel, to avoid any unlawful arrest and the detention of the vessel, under compelling circumstances issued P&amp;I club guarantee for US $ dollar 1,12,897.00 dated 18-3-2003, in favour of the plaintiff&#39;s for alleged cargo claim and another for US $ dollar 33,869.00, dated 18-3-2003, in favour of the Commissioner of Customs, Chittagong, for anticipated customs penalty through their P&amp;I Club, the Standard Steamship Owners Protection and Indemnity Association (Bermuda) Limited. It was stipulated in the said guarantee that the plaintiff&#39;s will be refrained from arresting or otherwise detaining and harassing the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; and the defendant shall pay on demand to the plaintiff such some as may be found to the due by final judgment of the High Court in Bangladesh or in Arbitration or by agreement between the parties. But despite such receiving the P&amp;I Club guarantee, the plaintiff instituted the instant suit and obtained an order of arrest of the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; on 29-3-2003, for which the owners of the vessel compelled to furnish bank guarantee being No. 864 /CG 6300483, dated 1-4-2003, issued by the Standard Chartered Bank; Dhaka, for an amount of Taka 85,25,636 in order to obtain the release of the vessel. &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; from arrest. Their is no short landing of the cargo CDSO from the carrier vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39;, rather an excess of 0.873 MT of the cargo was received by the plaintiffs as discharged from the vessel. The plaintiff&#39;s suffering of loss and damage is not true. Moreover, the declaration regarding quantum of CDSO made by the agent of the vessel Messrs Pacific Ocean Lines Ltd, the defendant No. 5, to the customs authority is a liability of the ship owners as such the plaintiff being mere consignee suffered no loss for the alleged customs penalty made against the declaring agent and therefore the claim raised by the plaintiff on the said Court is also not tenable in the eye of law as there is the P&amp;I Club guarantee issued in favour of the customs authority is still in existence. The plaintiff filed the instant suit for illegal gain and illegally detained and wrongfully arrested the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; and caused loss and injury to the defendant for which the defendant is entitled to recover a damage of US$ dollar 11,310.70, equivalent to Bangladeshi Taka 6,65,974.02, by way of counter claim on account of the illegal detention of the vessel at Chittagong Port for three days from 29-3-2003, up to 2-4-2003.&nbsp; 

 Joint Survey Reports 

 Ullage survey report and dry tank certificate 

 14. The PW 1 Md Aslam in his examination in chief narrated the plaint case who has been authorized by Bay Fishing Corporation Limited, the plaintiff No. 3, to depose on behalf of all the plaintiffs. The said authorization letter has been exhibited and marked as exhibit No. 1. The PW 1 filed the letters of credit of the plaintiffs, the bills of leading issued by the defendant vessel and other related documents in order to substantiate the fact of quantity of the imported CDSO. He has also submitted the shore tanks terminal survey report, exhibit 9 series, to show the short landing of 201.876 MT at bond temperature. He has also submitted the custom adjudication documents, being exhibit No. 15 series.&nbsp; 

 15. On cross-examination, the PW 1, upon seen the exhibit 8, stated that it is in writing in it that the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; arrived at Chittagong on 8-3-2003 at 04-12 hours and the cargo calculation was started at 14-20 hours and completed at 15-30 hours and the quantity of the cargo was found as per the ships figure i.e., 24,000.873 MT. The PW 1 also stated that the ullage survey was stated on 8-3-2003, at 14-20 hours and was completed at 15-30 hours which ascertained the quantity of the cargo at 24,000.873 MT. The PW 1 further stated in cross-examination that he attended the custom adjudication as representative of the local declaring agent of the vessel and the defendant No. 5 Pacific Ocean Line was the local declaring agent of the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39;, who further stated that the customs adjudication was directed against the local declaring agent Pacific Ocean Lines Ltd, Chittagong, the defendant No. 5. He further stated that the plaintiff was made a party to the custom adjudication.&nbsp; 

 16. PW 2 Kamrul Ahsan, representing the plaintiff No. 4 MM Vegetable Oil Products Ltd, proved the L/C, Exhibit 7, the bills of leading Exhibit Nos. 2, 7, 8 and 9, dated 27-1-2003. He also proved the survey report issued by Summit United Tank Terminal Limited, Exhibit-9(2), who stated that 23,798.304 MT. Cargo was discharged in the said tank terminal at bond temperature, and 201.699 MT CDSO was short landed. He also proved the claim letters and the heads of claim as appears in the plaint.&nbsp; 

 18. The DW 1 Md Halim Bhuiyan, in his examination-in-chief stated that the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; arrived at Chittagong Port on 8-3-2003, with the cargo of 24,000 MT of CDSO and a joint ullage survey was held on board the vessel prior to commencement of discharge of the cargo and the surveyor found 24,000.873 MT of CDSO on board the vessel and accordingly issued ullage survey report dated 8-3-2003, which he submitted and marked as Exhibit B. He stated that Port surveyor namely Captain AHM Khairul Anam appeared on behalf of the consignee and Captain QK Newaz, appeared on behalf of the owners P&amp;I Club, Captain MN Kamal, appeared on behalf of the ships declaring agent and the Chief Officer of the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; were present at the ullage survey and all of them signed the ullage survey report. He further stated that despite the fact that there was no short landing yet the owners of the vessel through their P&amp;I Club issued two letters of guarantee one in favour of the plaintiff with a condition that the plaintiff will be refrained from arresting and otherwise detaining and harassing the vessel. He further raised the counter claim against the plaintiff equivalent to an amount of Taka 6,65,974.00.&nbsp; 

 20. DW 2 Kazi Kamal Newaz, is a master mariner who is engaged in marine related survey including ullage survey of crude degummed Soyabean Oil from 1991. He, upon showing exhibit B stated that he was present at the ullage survey on behalf of the owner P&amp;l Club and Captain AHM Khairul Anam, appeared on behalf of the consignee, Captain MN Kamal, appeared on behalf of the declaring agent of the vessel and the Chief Officer of &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; were present at the joint ullage survey carried on the tank of the vessel on 8-3-2003 and all of them signed the ullage survey report of Exhibit B. He stated that 24,000.873 MT of CDSO was found in the tanks of the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; at the time of ullage survey and after the completion of the ullage survey the cargo was discharged from the vessel in the lighterage tanker vessel and thereafter upon completion of the discharge carried the dry tank inspection on 17-3-2003 and issued Dry tank report showing that the tanks were dry.&nbsp; 

 21. In his examination-in-cross the DW 2 stated that he could not recollect the name of the Captain of &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; who attended during the ullage survey. He further stated that during the ullage survey on 8-3-2003, the temperature shown in the sixth column of the report ranges from 27-30 degree Celsius.&nbsp; 

 30. The learned Advocate Mr SM Ahsanur Rahman, appearing on behalf of the plaintiffs while taken this Court through the different Exhibits filed by the parties developed his argument on these issues in manifold Court upon relying the shore tanks survey report Exhibit 9 and argued that admittedly the plaintiff imported 24,000 MT of CDSO which was loaded at the load Port Sanlorenzo, in the tanks of the defendant vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; and, as such, The defendant vessel is liable to discharge the same quantity of the cargo to the plaintiff. But the defendant since failed to deliver a quantity of 201.699 MT of CDSO, the plaintiff incurred loss mainly on three heads i.e. the price of the short landed goods, the customs Penalty and the business loss or the said quantity of the short landed cargo. 

 31. In elaborating the argument on these points the learned Advocate Mr SM Ahsanur Rahman, drawn the attention of this Court to the P&amp;I Club guarantee and argued that by issuing the P&amp;I Club guarantee the defendants admitted the short landing of the cargo which was ultimately found to be correct when the shore tank terminal survey was carried out. The learned Advocate Mr SM Ahsanur Rahman, refuted the claim of the defendant that 0.876 MT of CDSO was excess delivered to the plaintiff and argued that such claim of the defendant categorically proves that the defendants have not delivered the correct quantity of the CDSO as loaded in the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; which has been clearly proved from the customs survey which is more authentic survey report upon which the custom authority imposed Penalty upon the plaintiff for the short landing of the cargo through the adjudication order Exhibit No. 15(1).&nbsp; 

 32. The learned Advocate Mr SM Ahsanur Rahman, next argued that the ullage survey report was not prepared properly as no agent of the importer, the plaintiffs, were present at the time of conducting the ullage survey, although the Provision of Article 3 Rule 6 of the carriage of goods by sea Act, 1925 requires a joint survey. Therefore the defendant cannot relay upon the ullage survey report in order to substantiate their claim that excess quantity of CDSO was discharged and there was no short landing. The learned Advocate Mr SM Ahsanur Rahman, argued that the contract of carriage in this case is the bills of leading and Section 74 of the Contract Act will come into play when the question of compensation arises. In this respect he relied on the case of Elsar Navigation Water.&nbsp; 

 34. On the other hand the senior counsel Mr Fida M Kamal, appearing on behalf of the defendants along with Mr Muhammed Ohiullah, emphasized on the ullage survey report, so far the discharge of the CDSO is concerned and argued that the long standing practice in the carriage of goods by sea established the principal that when the fact of correct quantity of discharge of Liquid Cargo is in question, the ullage survey is the appropriate method for ascertaining the quantity of the cargo and not any other survey conducted elsewhere after the discharge as the liability of the carrier terminates just after the point of passing of the liquid through the ships rail i.e. when the delivery hose of the vessel is disconnected from the vessel tanks. Therefore, the ullage survey which is conducted before delivery of the cargo is the appropriate method to be considered by this Court in order to come to a finding that 24,000 MT of CDSO was discharged from the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39;. the learned Senior counsel Mr Fida M Kamal in this context referred the case of James Finlay PLC vs Member (Appeal &amp; Revision) Ministry of Finance, reported in 1990 BCR 194 and the case of Haque and Sons vs National Board of Revenue reported in 5 BLC 579 and also un unreported case of this Court being CA No. 46 of 1999, and the case of Lever Brothers Bangladesh Limited vs Botany Bay Shipping Company (Aust) Pvt. Ltd reported in 9 BLC 280.&nbsp; 

 36. The plaintiff brought this Admiralty Suit on the ground that 201.699 MT of CDSO was short landed out of 24,000 MT of imported CDSO from Argentina which was loaded on board the defendant vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; which arrived at Chittagong Port on 8-3-2003 and discharged the said cargo up to 17-3-2003 in the lighterage tanker vessel which in turn discharged the same in the shore tank of the plaintiffs. Thereafter, a survey was conducted in the shore tanks terminal in order to ascertain the discharge quantity of the CDSO and it was found that the said quantity of 201.699 MT cargo was short landed for which the plaintiff was saddled with the customs penalty for short landed cargo. The plaintiff further incurred loss due to the business loss it faced for the short landed cargo. It is the contention of the plaintiffs that to ascertain the discharge quantity of liquid cargo i, e; CDSO the shore tanks terminal survey report is the appropriate report to consider and since the customs authority upon conducting the shore tanks terminal survey found the short landing of the cargo and imposed the penalty under the provisions of section 156(1)(24)(2) of the customs Act 1961, the defendants are laible to compensate the plaintiff in all those count.&nbsp; 

 37. On the other hand, the defendants relied on the ullage survey report and argued that the only method of ascertaining the quantity of liquid cargo carried by a vessel is the ullage survey conducted at the port of discharge prior to commencement of the discharge of the liquid cargo. In this respect the reliance were made on the decision of the referred cases as decided by this Division.&nbsp; 

 &quot;In the light of the provision of clause 24 (ii) of the table under section 156(1) of the Customs Act 1969 and in the light of definition of &#39;conveyance&#39; as quoted above it can be said that the shortage, if any, has to be found in the conveyance which means that he shortage, if any, has to be found on board the vessel. Therefore, the tillage Survey Report on Board the vessel &#39;MT Botany Trojan&#39; shall prevail against the shore tank survey report in case of any difference in respect of shortage of cargo between the ullage Survey Report on board the vessel and the Shore Tank survey Report and that in case the shortage, if any, found in the ullage Survey on board the vessel is covered by the 1% admissible Ocean allowance then the carrier will not be responsible for any shortage of crude oil found in the Shore Tank Survey Report which is not covered by 1% admissible Ocean allowance and in such case no penalty can be imposed on the carrier or his agent. The respondents have not applied their mind to this aspect of the case.&quot;&nbsp; 

 &quot;It appears that the ullage survey was carried out on board the vessel &#39;MT Atlantic Antares&#39; on 1-4-1978 at the outer anchorage at Chittagong when the total quantity of cargo found on board at the time of survey was 40,445,53 crude oil as against the manifested quantity of 40,967 long tons of crude oil. Thus on the basis of ullage survey report there was shortage of 81.87 long tons of crude oil which was well within 1% Ocean allowance admissible. It has appears that the entire quantity of crude oil was discharged into the lightening vessels namely &#39;MT Banglar Alo&#39; and &#39;MT Banglar Kheya&#39; which was &#39;acknowledged by receipt of the total quantity of 41,024 long tons of crude oil. Therefore, it is evident that there was no shortage beyond the 1% Ocean allowance at conveyance, that is, when the crude oil was discharged by &#39;MT Atlantic Antares&#39; into the aforesaid lightening vessels. Dip reading of the shore tanks which allegedly shown shortage of 1,211.38 long tons of crude oil cannot be relied upon for imposing penalty in view of the provisions of section 156(1) of the Customs Act, 1969. In view of the definition of the word &#39;conveyance&#39; as made in the Customs Act, 1969 and for reasons as aforesaid when there is a conflict between ullage survey report and dip shore tank survey in respect of shortage of cargo the ullage survey report will prevail. Hence, all the actions taken by the respondents in imposing penalty in respect of shortage of cargo are without lawful authority and are of no legal effect&quot;.&nbsp; 

 &quot;It appears that an ullage survey was carried out prior to discharge of the cargo from the vessel &#39;MT Aktubinsk&#39; and a quantity of 1,923.457 metric tons of tallow was found as per the ullage survey report as opposed to the shore tank survey report and after giving 1% ocean allowance there remains co shortage whatsoever. Having considered the plaint, written statement, both oral and documentary evidence adduced by the parties it appears that a quantity of 5.754 metric tons tallow was found short as per the ullage survey report, but such alleged shortage is well within 1% Ocean allowance allowed to carrier in the carriage of bulk oil trade. Moreso, from the reported cases reported in 9 BLC 218;11 BLC 83; 5 BLC 579; BCR 1990 (HCD) 194 supports the case of the Appellants/ Defendant Nos. 1 and 2 wherein the Hon&#39;ble High Court Division has held that ullage survey must prevail over shore tank survey.&quot;&nbsp; 

 41. It appears from the aforesaid decisions that the question as to the mode of ascertaining the quantity of Liquid cargo at the time of delivery has been held to be the ullage survey method since at the time of conducting such survey the cargo remains in the tanks of the vessel and the ullage survey is being conducted&quot; before the commencement of discharge. It further appears that there is a logic behind such method of ascertaining the dischargeable Liquid cargo on the basis of ullage survey report since the cargo remains unbroken when the said survey is conducted. More so as the long line of decisions of this Court has already settled the point that the ullage survey report should be the basis of ascertaining the quantity of the dischargeable liquid cargo, this Court finds no scope to vary with the decisions as to such method of ascertainment of the quantity of the Liquid cargo.&nbsp; 

 42. It appears that on 8-3-2003, DW 2 Kazi Kamal Newaz, was present at the time of ullage survey conducted at the tanks of the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; and in his examination-in-chief he disclosed that Captain AHM Khairul Anam was the surveyor appointed by the consignee and there are other surveyor namely Mr MN Kamal present as the surveyor for the declaring agent of the vessel and the chief officer of the vessel were also present at the joint ullage survey carried out, whereupon they have issued the ullage survey report. It has been alleged by the plaintiffs that the said ullage survey report has not been conducted in presence of the consignee or any of the representative of the defendants but this Court finds that such an allegation has no substance since the plaintiff failed to examine the surveyor AHM Khairul Anam who had been the only witness to negative the contention of the defendants that the ullage survey was conducted in this presence as a surveyor of the consignee. Therefore, this Court finds that the ullage survey was conducted in presence of the surveyor Mr AHM Khairul Anam appointed by the consignee as proved by the DW 2 Kazi Kamal Newaz.&nbsp; 

 43. Accordingly, this Court finds that as the ullage survey report confirms that the vessel had the quantity of 24,000 MT of CDSO and the same quantity of CDSO was has been discharged by a the vessel in the lighterage tankers which carried the same to the shore tanks owned by the plaintiff and therefore the plaintiff&#39;s suffered no loss or damage on this Count.&nbsp; 

 46. It appears that the customs authority while adjudicating under Section 156(1)(40) of the Customs Act, 1969 considered the shore tank survey report Exhibit 9(1) and found that the vessel has delivered short of 201.699 MT of CDSO and accordingly imposed penalty under the said provisions of law upon the vessel declaring agent of the vessel, the defendant No. 5. This Court since already found that a long line of decision of this Court have decided as to the method of ascertaining the dischargeable quantity of the Liquid Cargo as the ullage survey report, this Court further finds that the customs authority upon misconception of law found that the vessel &#39;MT Trompeteros&#39; made a short delivery of 201.699 MT of CDSO and adjudicated under Section 156(1)(40) of the Customs Act 1969 against the declaring agent of the vessel and, as such, the same has no legal effect. The Customs authority if have realized any fine, is liable to refund it to the defendant No. 5.&nbsp;
